ZzzCharacter.cpp's per-frame effect switch, `case MODEL_GIANT: MonsterDieSandSmoke(o); break;` - and the function, in the same file:

    if (o->CurrentAction == MONSTER01_DIE && o->AnimationFrame >= 8.f && o->AnimationFrame < 9.f)
    {
        for (int i = 0; i < 20; i++)
        {
            Vector(1.f, 1.f, 1.f, o->Light);
            Vector(o->Position[0] + (float)(rand() % 64 - 32),
                o->Position[1] + (float)(rand() % 64 - 32),
                o->Position[2] + (float)(rand() % 32 - 16), Position);
            if (rand_fps_check(1))
                CreateParticle(BITMAP_SMOKE + 1, Position, o->Angle, o->Light, 1);
        }
    }

One thing, once: twenty puffs of smoke02 thrown in a single burst on key 8 of the eleven-key death, scattered over a 64-unit box round his feet, as he hits the ground. That is the whole of what he puts in the air - MODEL_GIANT is in no other effect case, so he raises no dust while he walks and breathes nothing.

Subtype 1 of BITMAP_SMOKE + 1, which is not the Dark Knight's subtype 0: ZzzEffectParticle's birth gives it LifeTime 40 rather than 32, zeroes the velocity the ordinary puff is thrown with, and jitters the height by 8 - then the mover pins every puff of this sheet to the terrain anyway, so what differs is a puff that sits where it was born, opening out for 1.6 s instead of 1.28 and leaning nowhere. Scale is the ordinary 0.32 to 0.64 growing 0.08 a frame, luminosity LifeTime / 32 on every channel and alpha, so it opens at more than full brightness and darkens to nothing. See client/core/Dust.Burst.

The window is a single key wide - 8.0 to 9.0 of a clip whose death plays at the ordinary 0.55, unslowed, because the multiplier stops before MONSTER01_DIE - and the emitter is a one-shot, so it fires once when the window opens and not once per frame inside it. rand_fps_check(1) is 'every reference frame', which inside a one-key window at 25 fps is once.
